  "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{\u2033}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{6}=4\\,\u27f9\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{2}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\omega t+{\u03d5}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\omega t+{\u03d5}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =2$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
